Good — going back to the hospital is exactly the right move.

One nuance: a “капельница” can be useful for fluids and other treatment, but **if this really is recurrent anaphylaxis with breathing/throat symptoms, IM adrenaline is the key first-line treatment**. IV fluids, antihistamines and steroids do not replace it. citeturn200873search2turn200873search26

The most useful thing for her or Alla to tell the staff is:

- severe allergic reaction earlier today;
- treated already;
- **symptoms have now returned**;
- history of a previous probable biphasic reaction;
- recurrent severe reactions with airway involvement;
- allergist specifically wanted acute tryptase during a future severe episode.

If they can draw tryptase without delaying treatment, **this is the right episode to do it** — ideally as early as possible, preferably within ~2 hours of symptom recurrence/onset. citeturn200873search24

And since she is with Alla now, that removes one of my biggest immediate concerns. I’d still strongly prefer that she **not be discharged home alone again quickly** if she has ongoing breathing symptoms or needs repeated adrenaline.

