If you mean **fever around 39°C**, that is a major new detail.

⚠️ **39°C fever is not a typical feature of anaphylaxis itself.** In the setting of repeated severe reactions, sore throat, and breathing problems, it raises concern that she may also have an acute infection or another inflammatory illness on top of the allergy problem. Infection can sometimes act as a cofactor that lowers the threshold for anaphylaxis, but the fever still needs to be evaluated on its own. citeturn533705search1turn533705search4

Because she has just had another severe reaction, **this combination deserves hospital-level assessment now**, not “wait and see at home,” especially if she has any of:
- difficulty breathing;
- confusion or unusual drowsiness;
- very fast breathing;
- marked weakness/shivering;
- worsening swelling;
- low blood pressure/faintness.

High fever plus breathing difficulty is also a reason clinicians need to consider serious infection, including sepsis, not just allergy. citeturn533705search0

