text:
I would not make the decision based on “it has been 12 hours since the first ambulance.”

The more relevant question is: **how long has she been completely stable since the most recent recurrence of symptoms?**

She apparently felt bad again around 7 AM, had marked generalized swelling, and needed additional treatment. In practical terms, that means the observation clock should be thought of from the **latest significant symptoms / latest treatment**, not just from the first episode last night.

Current anaphylaxis guidance recommends longer observation when there has been:
- a severe initial reaction;
- a previous biphasic reaction;
- severe respiratory involvement;
- repeated/recurrent symptoms;
- or difficulty responding safely if symptoms recur. citeturn349830view0

In fact, Resuscitation Council UK suggests **at least 12 hours after resolution of symptoms** for some higher-risk situations, including severe respiratory compromise; previous biphasic reaction is also specifically a reason for longer observation. citeturn349830view0

So given her history, **I would not encourage her to discharge herself simply because she feels mostly fine now.**

What she should do is ask the treating doctor directly:

> “У меня сегодня утром снова были выраженные отёки и мне потребовалось дополнительное лечение. С учётом повторных анафилаксий и прошлой двухфазной реакции, вы считаете, что мне уже безопасно домой, или лучше ещё остаться под наблюдением?”

If the doctor examines her and says she is safe for discharge, that's different. But I would want her to leave only after:
- breathing is back to her normal baseline;
- swelling is clearly resolving;
- she can stand/walk without dizziness;
- vital signs are reassuring;
- no new symptoms have appeared for a meaningful period;
- she has her EpiPen(s) immediately available;
- **someone can stay with her after discharge**, rather than her being alone.

Guidance specifically mentions checking for postural dizziness before discharge and having adequate supervision afterward. citeturn349830view0

The frequent urination after treatment is not inherently alarming. If they gave her substantial IV fluids, she may simply be eliminating that fluid; improvement in edema may contribute as well. I wouldn't interpret it diagnostically without knowing what she received.

Given the whole sequence you described — **another severe unexplained reaction, hospitalization, then further swelling requiring treatment this morning** — my bias would be toward letting the hospital decide she is ready rather than Lada deciding she feels “good enough” and leaving early.

And she should tell them about the **39°C fever for three days and sore throat before discharge**, if they don't already know. That needs its own assessment too.

