Your reasoning is understandable, and I’d split the difference rather than treating this as either “obviously fine, stay in bed” or “39.4 = emergency department.”

### What the previous COVID episode tells us

The fact that she previously had a very similar course — several days of high fever followed by fairly abrupt improvement — is genuinely somewhat reassuring. COVID can produce a miserable 5–7 day acute phase, and **39°C fever does not automatically mean pneumonia or severe COVID**.

But it doesn’t prove that this episode will follow the same trajectory.

The piece I pay more attention to is:

**day 6 + 39.4°C + she feels substantially worse today than yesterday.**

Current guidance treats fever lasting ≥5 days or symptoms that are worsening rather than improving as a reason for medical reassessment. citeturn265493search2turn265493search3

### What could a doctor actually do?

Your concern that they may simply say “COVID, drink water, paracetamol” is realistic. If she is oxygenating normally and has uncomplicated viral COVID, there may genuinely be no magic treatment to give her.

But the useful purpose of reassessment isn't necessarily to prescribe another drug. It is to check whether she is **still in the uncomplicated-COVID bucket**.

A competent assessment can look for:

- oxygen saturation and respiratory rate;
- dehydration and blood pressure/pulse;
- lung findings suggesting pneumonia;
- whether chest imaging is warranted;
- whether repeat bloodwork is warranted because the clinical picture has changed;
- secondary bacterial infection;
- another source of fever;
- whether her asthma/bronchospasm is flaring.

That distinction matters because treatment changes substantially if one of those things is found.

### What I would do today

If she is alert, breathing normally, drinking reasonably well, urinating, and has no significant chest pain, I think **staying in bed, hydrating, using paracetamol for comfort and monitoring her closely for the next several hours is reasonable**.

I would also get a pulse oximeter if you can. A resting saturation comfortably around 95–100% is reassuring. A repeated value **≤94% on room air**, especially with worsening symptoms, deserves medical assessment.

I wouldn't make her drag herself through town just to obtain a ceremonial second opinion while she has a 39.4 fever.

But because she says she is **clearly worse today**, I would set a fairly low threshold for reassessment rather than committing now to “she stays home until tomorrow no matter what.”

### ⚠️ I would call an ambulance / seek urgent care if

She develops:

- new or worsening shortness of breath;
- persistent chest pain/pressure;
- repeated SpO₂ ≤94%;
- confusion or unusually difficult awakening;
- fainting/severe unsteadiness;
- inability to drink or repeated vomiting;
- markedly reduced urination;
- severe weakness that is getting worse;
- or she simply becomes strikingly more ill.

Those are recognized warning signs of respiratory-virus complications. citeturn265493search0turn265493search1

### One useful checkpoint

I'd pay particular attention to **tonight and tomorrow morning**.

If she follows the pattern of her previous infection and the fever finally breaks and she begins feeling distinctly better, great.

If tomorrow becomes **day 7 with continuing 39-ish fever**, or she keeps deteriorating despite normal breathing, I would want another medical assessment even without low oxygen. At that point the probability that there is something worth looking for besides straightforward COVID becomes higher.

So I don't think your “stay in bed + ambulance if really bad” advice was unreasonable. I would just add **active monitoring and a time limit** rather than assuming the previous COVID episode guarantees that this one will resolve the same way.

