text:
The photo shows about **152/94 mmHg with pulse 121/min**.

The blood pressure is elevated, but **152/94 is not a hypertensive emergency by itself**. The usual emergency range is >180/120, particularly when accompanied by chest pain, shortness of breath, neurologic symptoms, etc. citeturn462158search0turn462158search1

What concerns me more is **a resting pulse around 121**, especially because she says this happened last night too. Fever itself can absolutely cause sinus tachycardia, as can dehydration, pain, anxiety, infection and some medications. citeturn496350search2turn496350search9

### What I would do right now

Have her lie/sit quietly for 10–15 minutes, then measure again correctly:
- no talking;
- feet down/back supported if sitting;
- arm supported at heart level;
- take 2 measurements a few minutes apart.

At the same time, check:
- temperature;
- **SpO₂**, if she has an oximeter;
- whether she has chest pain, shortness of breath, dizziness/faintness, confusion, or an unusually irregular heartbeat.

She should drink fluids if she can, and use plain paracetamol if another dose is due and she can safely take it. If the heart rate falls substantially as the fever comes down and she hydrates, that would fit a physiologic response to the infection.

### ⚠️ When I wouldn't just watch it at home

I would seek urgent assessment tonight if:
- pulse remains around **120+ at complete rest** despite resting/hydration/fever treatment, especially if it is rising;
- she develops chest pain or pressure;
- new/worsening breathlessness;
- fainting or near-fainting;
- confusion or extreme weakness;
- SpO₂ repeatedly **<94%**;
- or BP approaches **180/120**. citeturn496350search0turn496350search5turn462158search0

Given **day 6 of COVID, 39.4°C earlier today, feeling worse than yesterday, and now recurrent nocturnal tachycardia**, I wouldn't dismiss this as “just anxiety.” It may simply be fever + dehydration, but persistent resting tachycardia is one of the things a doctor can objectively evaluate with an ECG, oxygen saturation, examination, and perhaps repeat electrolytes/CBC.

One extra consideration: if she has started the **dexamethasone** prescribed yesterday, tell me the dose. Steroids can contribute to insomnia, sympathetic activation and sometimes elevated blood pressure, although they wouldn't be my first explanation for HR 121 during a 39°C infection. Also avoid multi-ingredient cold remedies/decongestants tonight—they can push heart rate and blood pressure higher. citeturn462158search10

